Fork notes (this entry, markdown-render-speed-02)
--------------------------------------------------
In THIS entry's copy, marked.esm.js is a performance fork of the file above.
No rendering rule, regex meaning, token shape, or output string changes;
each change only skips work whose outcome is already decided, so the HTML
stays byte-for-byte identical (verified 720/720 on the practice set):

1. Block lexer dispatch (Lexer.blockTokens): before trying a block
   tokenizer, look at the first characters (after up to three leading
   spaces). space/code need a leading space or tab; fences need a backtick
   or tilde; heading a '#'; hr one of '-', '_', '*'; blockquote a '>';
   list a bullet or digit; html a '<'; def a '['; table a '-' somewhere
   in the second line; lheading a line starting (after up to three
   spaces) with '=' or '-'. Every guard is a conservative superset of
   the rule it guards: it may let a tokenizer run and fail, but it never
   stops one that could have matched, and the original attempt order is
   preserved. A skipped tokenizer is one whose anchored regex was
   guaranteed to fail at this position.

2. Inline lexer dispatch + masking skips (Lexer inline loop, the
   masking helper fe, and the mask builder): inline tokenizers are
   attempted only when the first character can start them — escape and
   br need a backslash (br also a space), tag and autolink a '<', link
   and reflink a '[' or '!', emStrong a '*' or '_', codespan a backtick,
   del a '~', and url one of its literal starts (www., mailto:, xmpp:,
   http(s)://, ftp://, or an address run ending in '@'). The emphasis
   mask scan returns the all-absent result immediately when the source
   contains none of '*', '_', '~'. In the mask builder, the
   punctuation-outside-code pass is skipped when the source contains
   neither a backtick nor a backslash (the only characters its pattern
   can match), and the block-skip pass is skipped when the source
   contains none of '[', '`', '<' (the only characters its alternatives
   can start with). A skipped pass is one whose global replace would
   have matched nothing, i.e. the identity.

3. List tokenizer line scanning (Tokenizer.list): the first line of a
   string is taken with indexOf('\n') + slice instead of
   split('\n', 1)[0], which allocated an array for a string that was
   then thrown away; the substring produced is the same. Tab expansion
   of a line (the leading-whitespace replace, and the whole-line tab
   replace used in a search test) now runs only when the line actually
   begins with whitespace or contains a tab, respectively — in every
   other case the replace was guaranteed to return its input unchanged.
